nd explain why briefly.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies see different inputs, so the other's action isn't a child of my node under strict A-FDT. Treating the other's node as a sibling with its own prior, my best response to any cooperation probability is to defect. That makes A-FDT recommend defecting, unless the other copy's action can be argued to depend on mine in some other way.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's computation tracks mine, which under strict A-FDT it doesn't, since its node stays fixed as a sibling. Defecting is the unique fixed point here, though policy-FDT would cooperate, and the essay frames this case on purpose. I'll go with defecting and explain why briefly.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9738,"completion_tokens":655,"total_tokens":10393,"cost":0.052052,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.052052,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.038952,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0131},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":321,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}